{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · many standpoints","loop":40,"ease":"","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.101821636,-0.416608909,0.0652652727,-0.235607455]},{"t":0.6,"box":[-0.101821636,-0.416608909,0.0652652727,-0.235607455]},{"t":0.73,"box":[-0.0805771636,-0.429668073,0.114968073,0.343246255]},{"t":0.8,"box":[-0.0805771636,-0.429668073,0.114968073,0.343246255]},{"t":0.84,"box":[-0.101821636,-0.416608909,0.0652652727,-0.235607455]}],"stops":[0.2,0.4,0.6,0.8],"beats":[{"title":"One eye: a direction is a line","text":"Close one eye and look at something. It comes to you as a direction: a line from your eye through it to whatever lies beyond. **Every point on that line looks the same.** The faint dots, half as far and twice as far, are seen exactly where the target is.\n\nOne standpoint gives directions, and the corner of each; it gives no distance."},{"title":"Two eyes: two lines cross","text":"Open the other eye, a baseline away. From there the target is a second line, and the two lines **cross at the target**. The crossing is a place.\n\nThe length every place is found in is the baseline itself, which you have never measured: here the target is 2.8 half-baselines out."},{"title":"Parallax has a corner","text":"Read each direction only to a step, and each line becomes a narrow wedge. Where the two wedges cross is everything the pair cannot tell apart: the patch.\n\nThe circle on the baseline is **parallax's corner** (Thales): there the lines of sight meet square, and the patch is round. Nearer or farther it stretches along the line of sight, alike at *k* and 1/*k* half-baselines. [Parallax has a corner, §4](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/06-many-standpoints.md)"},{"title":"Past the far edge, a ray","text":"Farther out the patch stretches as the square of the distance. At the far edge, where the whole parallax is no more than the two eyes' steps together, the outer lines no longer meet at all, and **the place opens back into a ray**.\n\nPast its band, parallax gives what one eye gives, a direction. Each eye's step here is a coarse 0.1 rad so the patches can be seen, which puts the edge about 10 half-baselines out; real eyes reach about 650 m."},{"title":"One length writes them all","text":"Everything so far was in baselines: free, and the same for any pair of eyes. Carry in one amount, the baseline's length, and every place is written in it.\n\nTwo eyes 6.5 cm apart put this target 9.2 cm away; Earth's orbit, two astronomical units across, puts the same picture's target 2.8 AU away. **The picture does not change; only the one amount does.** [The essay](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/06-many-standpoints.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.681818182,0.681818182,-0.681818182,-0.681818182],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":1},{"morph":7},{"morph":12},{"morph":4},{"morph":3},{"morph":9},{"morph":5},{"morph":6},{"morph":10},{"morph":2},{"morph":8},{"morph":11},{"morph":14},{"morph":15},{"morph":16},{"morph":17},{"morph":18},{"morph":19},{"morph":20},{"morph":21},{"morph":13},{"morph":22},{"morph":23},{"morph":24},{"morph":25},{"morph":26},{"morph":27},{"morph":28},{"morph":29},{"morph":30},{"morph":31},{"morph":32},{"morph":33},{"morph":34},{"morph":35},{"morph":36},{"morph":37},{"morph":38},{"morph":39},{"morph":40},{"morph":41},{"morph":42},{"morph":43},{"morph":44},{"morph":45},{"morph":46},{"morph":47},{"morph":48},{"morph":49},{"morph":50},{"morph":51},{"morph":52},{"morph":53},{"morph":54}],"morphs":[{"exact":true,"open":true,"holes":null,"keys":[{"x":[-0.0272727273,0.0163636364],"y":[-0.376363636,-0.376363636],"r":[0,0],"c":[null,null]}],"fill":null,"inPlace":true,"lag":0,"fx":null,"own":{"parent":-1,"parentAt":null,"follow":null,"look":-1,"lookRef":0,"phase":0,"rate":1,"spin":0,